A||||9||Cooter||Power seat problems||||1||
Z||000000||Cooter||12-25-1999||10:41 PM||||I finally found a couple of bad connections and a blown fuse that had most of my interior electronics from working. Now the buttons on the drivers seat cause a buzzing noise in the seat, but nothing else is happening. It looks like one is for lumbar support and the other is for seat sides to "squeeze" you in. I know nothing about this seat setup, so any input is appreciated.||||reg||1
Z||000001||mshill||12-25-1999||11:38 PM||||Be real careful with those power seat motors. In my wifes GMC Safari one of the motors had a low impedance (close to shorting out) and was killing batteries, then one day my wife complained of a burning rubber smell so I went out to find the connector and wiring insulation going to the motor were literally smoking.<P>If there is a problem with the motor or swithes you may have a short, and you will be very lucky if the battery runs out of juice before the car burns to the ground.||||reg||10
Z||000002||batboy||12-26-1999||07:25 AM||||I might first take the seats out and test the motors with a good extra car battery and a couple wire hooked up. That would narrow down the problem search. You could also use a ohm meter and operate the switches while watching the meter. That buzzing noise when dealing with electric automotive relays and switches usually means weak voltage possibly from a bad ground, short in the wire, or a loose connection. I've seen a bad fuse do it too. I know you checked fuses once, you might try looking again. I've seen those style fuse burn out down by the plug-in spade before and it was not very noticeable that it was bad.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||12-26-1999||01:48 PM||||definatly make sure there's no short.......<P>buzzing motor in a seat usually means the motor unit is bad. either it's jammed or it's stripped a gear. usually the motor units aren't repairable.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Bill Osmer||12-26-1999||06:30 PM||||Here's another thought... if I remember about lumbar seats, they usually are nothing more than an air bag that needs an air pump to inflate them. Some of the air pumps that I've heard do sound like "buzzing" because they're pumping, not moving mechanically. Perhaps, since you've fixed everything else, you actually have the lumbar fixed, too, but don't realize it. Are they inflating????<p>[This message has been edited by Bill Osmer (edited 12-26-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cooter||12-26-1999||06:52 PM||||All that happens when you press the buttons is a humming/buzzing noise- It does sound like an air pump- but no movement from the seat whatsoever. I wonder if the inflatable "bag" is just rotten and leaks. Someone in another thread had mentioned using late model Trans Am seats with the power lumbar, I wonder if they use the same type inflation system.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||12-26-1999||07:30 PM||||an air system could have one of 3 main problems.<P>A valve or air line has failed. the thing is trying to inflate but the valve or air line is letting the air just flow out.<P>The pump has failed. the motor works but the seal is gone so it's not but making noise.<P>the air bag has failed.<P>An airling can likely be fixed. a valve just may be dirty. the pump may be repairable, I haven't seen one for Fiero so I can't say for sure. The airbag may also be repairable or be similar enough to one in another GM car to be replaced.||||reg||1
